  "full_response": "Consensus algorithms are vital in distributed systems for achieving agreement on a single data value among distributed processes or nodes, ensuring consistency and coordination even in the presence of faults. The Raft consensus algorithm is particularly known for its simplicity and understandability. Here’s an overview of how Raft operates:\n\n### Key Concepts in Raft:\n\n1. **Leader Election**: Raft ensures there is a single leader that manages the log replication process. Periodically, each node in the system can become a candidate and request votes from other nodes to become the leader.\n\n2. **Log Replication**: Once a leader is elected, it accepts client requests, which are turned into log entries. These entries are replicated across follower nodes to ensure consistency of the replicated log entries.\n\n3. **Membership Changes**: Raft can also manage changes in the set of participating nodes in a cluster, often required in dynamic systems where nodes might fail and need to be replaced.\n\n### Raft Phases:\n\nRaft operates in several distinct but straightforward phases:\n\n#### Leader Election:\n- **Follower State**: Nodes begin in the follower state. If a follower does not receive a heartbeat from the leader within a randomized timeout, it transitions to a candidate state and starts a new election term by incrementing its term number.\n- **Candidate State**: The candidate requests votes from other nodes. A candidate that receives a majority of votes becomes the leader. If no majority is received, the candidate becomes a follower again.\n- **Leader Term Increment**: Each time a leader loses contact with a majority of nodes or a node is promoted to a leader, a new term begins.\n\n#### Log Replication:\n- **Leader Role**: The leader accepts client requests, appends them as new entries to its log, and replicates these entries to follower nodes.\n- **AppendEntries RPC**: The leader sends `AppendEntries` RPCs to followers to replicate log entries. This RPC can act both as a log replication tool and as a heartbeat mechanism to continually assert leadership.\n- **Commit**: Once an entry is safely replicated on a majority of the servers (including itself), the leader considers the entry committed. It will then apply the entry to its state machine and notify followers that they may apply the entry.\n\n#### Safety Mechanisms:\n- **Log Matching Property**: Raft ensures that if two logs contain an entry with the same index and term, then the logs are identical in all preceding entries.\n- **Leader Completeness**: A leader must have all previous log entries replicated on its state before it can append a new entry.\n\n",
